<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      docs/plan/03 § src — the .Contracts split. The sending domain's grain interfaces, wire types and
      seams: the channel abstraction, the message a caller hands us, the suppression entry, the
      template and its typed parameters, the spend reservation, and the webhook envelope a delivery
      receipt arrives in.

      ⚠ ONE PACKAGE, Microsoft.Orleans.Sdk — the code generator plus Orleans.Serialization, no
      hosting. CyberCloud.Identity, CyberCloud.Monitor and the billing dunning path reference this
      assembly to send, and must not thereby acquire a silo. CyberCloud.Communication — the
      implementation — is where hosting starts.

      ⚠ NO TWILIO, NO META CLOUD API, NO SES, AND NOT BY OVERSIGHT. IChannelProvider is the seam;
      docs/plan/17 § The channel abstraction names the implementations and this repository ships none
      of them. There are no credentials to test against and no network to reach, and a half-real
      carrier client is worse than an honest refusal — it is a thing that looks sent. The refusing
      seams live in CyberCloud.Communication/Providers and each says what a real implementation owes.

      ⚠ WHY THIS DOES NOT REFERENCE CyberCloud.ResourceManager.Contracts, even though SecretRef lives
      there. CarrierSecretRef is declared here instead, exactly as CyberCloud.Identity.Contracts
      declares VaultSecretRef, and for the same reason: a module's wire surface should not drag the
      resource manager's in behind it. The three types are the same shape by agreement, which
      docs/plan/00 § Non-negotiables states once ("secrets are SecretRef handles resolved at the data
      plane") and each module spells in its own vocabulary.

      ⚠ WHY THIS DOES NOT REFERENCE CyberCloud.Tenancy.Contracts. Sending is rate- and spend-limited
      per tenant, per channel, per day (docs/plan/17 § The parts that are actually the work), which
      looks like quota and is not: QuotaMeter is a vocabulary of resources a subscription HOLDS, and
      a message is not held. Reaching for IQuotaGrain would put a durable reservation on the send
      path — see ISendLimitGrain for the split that avoids it.
    -->

    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Orleans.Sdk"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Core/CyberCloud.Core.csproj"/>
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Core.Contracts/CyberCloud.Core.Contracts.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup Label="CyberCloud.Analyzers — see CyberCloud.Core.csproj for why this is not a reference">
        <!--
          CC1003 ([Alias] on every wire type) and CC1005 (the IdempotencyKey suppression, carrying its
          argument) are both live here. CC1005 is the one that matters most in this assembly: BYO
          carrier credentials are the whole reason CarrierSecretRef exists.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Analyzers/CyberCloud.Analyzers.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
